from _common import BOOK, CLINIC

A = {
'fever-comparison-2569': dict(
  title='How Many Days of High Fever Before Seeing a Doctor? Telling Dengue, Flu and an Ordinary Fever Apart',
  excerpt='How an ordinary fever, flu and dengue differ, and which danger signs mean you must go to hospital immediately. Read this before deciding when to see a doctor.',
  seo_title='High Fever: When to See a Doctor? Dengue vs Flu vs Common Fever | HappiWell',
  seo_description='How an ordinary fever, flu and dengue differ, and the danger signs that need a hospital immediately. Know when to see a doctor. HappiWell Medical Clinic, Thung Khru, Bangkok.',
  blocks=[
    'When your child or you get a fever, the first thought is often “it’s just a cold — rest and it will pass”. But in the rainy season, a high fever is not always just a cold. It could be flu or dengue, and each needs different care and has different warning signs. This article helps you tell them apart and know when you really need to see a doctor.',
    'What does an ordinary fever look like?',
    'A fever from a common cold virus usually comes on gradually, is not very high, and comes with a runny nose, cough and a slightly sore throat. It usually improves within 2–3 days with nothing more than rest and enough fluids.',
    'What does flu look like?',
    'Flu comes on much more suddenly than an ordinary fever, usually with a high fever straight away, aches all over the body and exhaustion so bad you can hardly get up, along with cough, sore throat and runny nose. It differs from an ordinary fever in how severe it is and how quickly it starts. High-risk groups such as young children, older adults and people with long-term conditions may develop complications such as pneumonia. (See <a href="/articles/flu-vaccine-2569">our flu vaccine article</a> for more.)',
    'What does dengue look like, and what do people often get wrong?',
    'Dengue usually starts with a sudden high fever (above 39–40°C) together with at least 2 of the following: severe headache, pain behind the eyes, muscle and joint pain, nausea and vomiting, or a rash.',
    'The most dangerous misunderstanding is that <b>a falling fever does not always mean you are getting better</b>. The critical phase of dengue usually occurs around days 3–7 of the illness, often exactly when the fever starts to drop. Many people think they are recovering and stop watching for symptoms, when in fact this is the time to be most careful: about 1 in 20 people with symptoms may develop severe dengue during this phase. (See <a href="/articles/dengue-vaccine-2569">our dengue vaccine article</a> for more.)',
    'Danger signs: go to hospital immediately',
    'Whatever kind of fever you suspect, go to hospital immediately, without waiting for an appointment, if you have any of the following:',
    ['Severe stomach pain or tenderness', 'Persistent vomiting (3 or more times in 24 hours)', 'Unusual bleeding, such as nosebleeds, bleeding gums, vomiting blood or black stools', 'Drowsiness, extreme tiredness, restlessness or unusually cold skin', 'Rapid or difficult breathing', 'A high fever for more than 2–3 days that does not come down, or a fever that has dropped but other symptoms are getting worse'],
    'How many days before seeing a doctor?',
    'If the fever is not very high and there are no danger signs, you can watch it at home for the first 1–2 days. But if a high fever lasts more than 2 days without improving, or you have symptoms that suggest flu or dengue, see a doctor for a clear diagnosis. Do not take NSAID painkillers (such as ibuprofen) on your own until dengue has been ruled out, as they may increase the risk of bleeding.',
    'HappiWell Clinic: fever checks and quick diagnosis',
    'If you have a high fever and are not sure what it is, HappiWell Medical Clinic in Thung Khru is ready to have a doctor diagnose it and advise on the right care — no need to travel to a big hospital. Booking is easy, with no long wait.',
    BOOK,
    'Frequently asked questions',
    '<b>My fever has gone down. Am I better?</b><br>Not necessarily — especially with dengue, where the fever dropping often coincides with the critical phase. Keep watching for other symptoms such as stomach pain, vomiting or unusual bleeding.',
    '<b>Is fever in young children different from adults?</b><br>Young children are at higher risk from both flu and dengue. Watch them especially closely and see a doctor sooner than you would for an adult if the fever does not come down within 1–2 days.',
    '<b>How are dengue and flu told apart?</b><br>The doctor will take a history and examine you, and in some cases do a blood test to confirm. They cannot be reliably told apart from outward symptoms alone.',
    '<b>Can I take fever medicine while waiting to see a doctor?</b><br>Paracetamol can be used to reduce fever according to the label, but avoid NSAIDs such as ibuprofen or aspirin until dengue has been ruled out.',
    'Summary',
    'An ordinary fever, flu and dengue have different clues, but one rule applies to all: if a high fever lasts more than 2–3 days without improving, or you have danger signs such as severe stomach pain, persistent vomiting, unusual bleeding or drowsiness, see a doctor immediately. With dengue in particular, the danger often comes just as the fever starts to fall, so do not relax just because the fever has dropped.',
    'References',
    None,
    '<i>This article is for general information and is not personal medical advice. If you have a high fever or any danger signs, see a doctor immediately.</i>',
  ]),

'free-condoms-2569': dict(
  title='HIV and Sexually Transmitted Infections in Thailand: What Is the Situation Now?',
  excerpt='An update on HIV and STIs in Thailand in 2026, how to protect yourself, and free condoms in all sizes at HappiWell Clinic, Thung Khru.',
  seo_title='HIV & STIs in Thailand 2026: Latest Situation | HappiWell Clinic',
  seo_description='An update on HIV and sexually transmitted infections in Thailand in 2026, how to protect yourself, and free condoms in all sizes at HappiWell Medical Clinic, Thung Khru, Bangkok.',
  blocks=[
    'News about HIV and sexually transmitted infections often features alarming big numbers, but those numbers are often missing context and can be misleading. This article looks at verified data on the real situation, what is genuinely worrying, and how you can protect yourself.',
    'HIV in Thailand: are new infections rising or falling?',
    'In early 2026, a widely reported figure said Thailand had about 13,000 new HIV infections. The Department of Disease Control later explained that this figure counted people newly diagnosed and registered that year, which does not mean they were all infected that year — some may have been infected years earlier but only recently tested.',
    'The Department of Disease Control’s modelled estimates of actual new infections each year show a <b>steady decline</b>: from 9,230 in 2022 to 9,083 in 2023 and 8,124 in 2024. The total number of people living with HIV nationwide is estimated at around 550,000–570,000.',
    'What is really worrying is not new HIV infections, but other STIs among young people',
    'Although new HIV infections are trending down, data on other sexually transmitted infections among young people is clearly worrying. Over the past 5 years:',
    ['<b>Syphilis among young people rose 2.7-fold</b>, from 4,326 to 11,813 cases', '<b>Gonorrhoea rose 2.5-fold</b>, from 3,912 to 9,897 cases'],
    'These figures show that unprotected sex is still a major problem among young people, even though awareness of prevention has grown.',
    'What does the example of Chiang Mai show?',
    'The Chiang Mai Provincial Public Health Office reports more than 23,900 cumulative HIV cases since surveillance began, with an average of around 700 new cases a year — about 2 a day — and young people aged 15–24 are the most worrying group. This high cumulative figure does not mean a new outbreak, but it shows that new infections continue every year, and Chiang Mai has a highly mobile population, so close surveillance is needed.',
    'Why do some people still not know they are infected?',
    'A key problem public health experts always stress is that many people live without knowing their infection status, because there are no clear symptoms early on, so they may pass it on without realising. The good news is that with early diagnosis and continuous antiretroviral treatment, the virus can be suppressed to undetectable levels, which means it is not passed on to others, and people can live completely normal lives.',
    'Thai citizens can currently get a free HIV test twice a year at a nearby health facility, or get a self-test kit through the Pao Tang app (Health Wallet menu &gt; preventive health benefits), with preliminary results in 20 minutes.',
    'Condoms protect more than you might think',
    'Of all prevention methods, condoms are the only one that protects against both HIV and other sexually transmitted infections at the same time, such as syphilis, gonorrhoea and HPV. PrEP, by contrast, protects only against HIV. So condoms are still recommended every time you have sex, even if you are using another method of protection.',
    'If you have recently been at risk of HIV exposure, or are interested in preventive medicine, read more about PEP and PrEP in <a href="/articles/pep-prep-2569">our article on the difference between PEP and PrEP</a>.',
    'Free condoms in all sizes at HappiWell Clinic',
    f'{CLINIC} in Thung Khru gives out condoms <b>free of charge in all sizes</b>. Just drop by the clinic — there is no cost — and feel free to talk to a doctor about sexual health. No need to feel embarrassed or wait for symptoms before coming in. Prevention is always better than cure.',
    'For more information, message us on LINE or call HappiWell Clinic. We are open every day: Monday–Friday 08:00–18:00 and Saturday–Sunday 08:00–12:00.',
    'Frequently asked questions',
    '<b>I have only one partner. Do I still need protection?</b><br>Yes. Risk can come from your own or your partner’s past, if your partner has never been tested. Testing and protection do not mean you do not trust each other — they are a way of looking after your health together.',
    '<b>Does an HIV test hurt, and how soon do I get the result?</b><br>It is a blood test or a self-test kit, with preliminary results in about 20 minutes.',
    '<b>I have no symptoms at all. Do I need to be tested?</b><br>Yes. HIV and many other STIs often cause no clear symptoms early on. Testing is the only way to know your real status.',
    '<b>Do I have to buy anything else to get free condoms at the clinic?</b><br>No. Just drop by and pick them up — no conditions.',
    'Summary',
    'According to the Department of Disease Control, new HIV infections in Thailand are trending down. What is still genuinely worrying is the clear rise in other sexually transmitted infections among young people, and the many people who do not know their status. Regular testing and using a condom every time remain the most effective protection. Pick up free condoms at HappiWell Clinic at no cost.',
    'References',
    ['<a href="https://policywatch.thaipbs.or.th/article/life-272">Thai PBS PolicyWatch – Gen-Z HIV infections rising: is the epidemic coming back? (Thai)</a>', '<a href="https://www.thaipbs.or.th/news/content/508800">Thai PBS – Chiang Mai monitors HIV, gives treatment within 24 hours of diagnosis (Thai)</a>', '<a href="https://www.thaihealth.or.th/">Thai Health Promotion Foundation – Ministry of Public Health releases Thai HIV statistics (Thai)</a>'],
    '<i>This article is for general information and is not personal medical advice.</i>',
  ]),

'contraception-injection-2569': dict(
  title='Which Contraception Suits You? The Pill, the Injection or an IUD (2026 Update)',
  excerpt='A comparison of popular contraceptive methods — the pill, injections, IUDs and condoms — and who each suits, plus the price of the 3-month contraceptive injection at HappiWell Clinic, Thung Khru.',
  seo_title='Which Contraception Is Best for You? Pill, Injection or IUD (2026) | HappiWell',
  seo_description='Compare popular contraceptive methods — the pill, injections, IUDs and condoms — and who each suits, plus the 3-month injection price at HappiWell Medical Clinic, Thung Khru, Bangkok.',
  blocks=[
    'Every time you walk into a pharmacy or clinic, there are so many contraceptive options that it is hard to choose: pills, injections, IUDs, implants and condoms. Each has its pros and cons. No method is “the best” for everyone — only the one that best fits your lifestyle and body.',
    'This article compares widely used contraceptive methods, how effective they really are and who they suit, and answers a common question: can you get contraception for free?',
    'Why choose what suits you, not just what a friend recommends?',
    'Each method works differently. Some rely on you being consistent, such as taking a pill at the same time every day; others are given once and last for months or years without you having to think about them. This makes a big difference to how effective each method is in real life, even though some are almost 100% effective in theory.',
    'Choosing a method that does not suit you — such as the pill when you often forget to take tablets — is a leading cause of preventable unplanned pregnancies.',
    'Comparing popular contraceptive methods',
    '<b>The contraceptive pill</b><br>Must be taken at the same time every day. Used perfectly, it is very effective, but in real life, where people sometimes forget, about 7% of users have an unplanned pregnancy each year. It suits people who are confident they will not forget, and who also want to regulate their periods or reduce acne.',
    '<b>The 3-month contraceptive injection (DMPA)</b><br>One injection gives 3 months of contraception, with no daily pill to remember. In real-life use, about 4% of users have an unplanned pregnancy. It suits people with busy lives who forget easily or find daily pills inconvenient. Good to know: periods may become irregular or stop while using it, and after stopping it may take some time before fertility returns to normal.',
    '<b>Intrauterine devices (IUDs) and contraceptive implants</b><br>The most effective of all reversible methods, with an unplanned pregnancy rate below 1%, because they do not depend on the user at all. Inserted once, they last from 3 to 10 years depending on the type. They suit people who want long-term contraception without frequent attention.',
    '<b>Condoms</b><br>Less effective in real-life use than hormonal methods and IUDs (up to about 13–18% unplanned pregnancies a year if not used consistently), but they are the only method here that also protects against sexually transmitted infections, so they are often recommended together with another method for double protection.',
    'Who is the 3-month contraceptive injection especially suitable for?',
    'Of all the options, the 3-month injection is very popular with working people because:',
    ['You do not have to think about it every day like the pill — just remember your next injection date', 'It suits people who had side effects from combined hormonal pills (oestrogen + progestin), because the injection contains a progestin only', 'It reduces menstrual bleeding in many people, so it suits those with heavy periods'],
    'One thing to consider: if you plan to have a baby soon, it may not be your first choice, because ovulation can take several months to return after stopping. Talk to a doctor to plan ahead.',
    'Can you get contraception for free? What does the Gold Card cover?',
    'Thailand’s National Health Security Office (NHSO) provides free contraception for people of reproductive age under the 30 Baht Gold Card, covering the pill, injections, implants, IUDs and emergency contraception to prevent unplanned pregnancy. Ask about using your rights at the service unit near home where you are registered.',
    'Where to get the 3-month injection, and how much does it cost?',
    'Prices for the 3-month contraceptive injection (DMPA) vary between clinics, depending on the location and whether a consultation is included.',
    f'At {CLINIC} in Thung Khru, the 3-month contraceptive injection costs <b>100 baht</b>, with a doctor’s consultation before every injection to check it suits your body and health history. Booking is easy, with no long wait.',
    'To book your contraceptive injection, message us on LINE or call HappiWell Clinic. We are open every day: Monday–Friday 08:00–18:00 and Saturday–Sunday 08:00–12:00.',
    'Frequently asked questions',
    '<b>Does the 3-month injection really cause weight gain?</b><br>It is a side effect in some people, and how much varies from person to person. Talk to a doctor if you are concerned.',
    '<b>Do I need to wait for my period before my first injection?</b><br>Doctors usually recommend having it early in your cycle to be sure you are not already pregnant. Talk to a doctor to plan the right day.',
    '<b>Can I get pregnant straight after stopping?</b><br>Everyone’s body responds differently. Some people ovulate again quickly; for others it may take several months. If you are planning a pregnancy, talk to a doctor in advance.',
    '<b>Does the contraceptive injection protect against STIs?</b><br>No. It only prevents pregnancy. To protect against sexually transmitted infections as well, use condoms too.',
    'Summary',
    'No contraceptive method is “the best” for everyone. Each has pros and cons depending on your lifestyle and health. The 3-month injection is a convenient choice for people who do not want to worry about remembering a daily pill. If you are not sure which method suits you, talk to a doctor to assess your health and goals before deciding.',
    'References',
    ['<a href="https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/73/rr/rr7303a1.htm">CDC – U.S. Selected Practice Recommendations for Contraceptive Use, 2024</a>', '<a href="https://www.guttmacher.org/fact-sheet/contraceptive-effectiveness-united-states">Guttmacher Institute – Contraceptive Effectiveness in the United States</a>', '<a href="https://www.prd.go.th/th/content/category/detail/id/9/iid/184921">Government Public Relations Department – Free contraception for people of reproductive age under the Gold Card (Thai)</a>'],
    '<i>This article is for general information and is not personal medical advice. Always talk to a doctor before choosing a contraceptive method.</i>',
  ]),
}
